Structured outputs under the batch queue: a grammar-constrained request makes the engine
wait for the previous step's tokens only once its grammar applies, so a request that reasons
before it answers (the app's memory extraction) keeps every decode step pipelined until its
reasoning ends. While it reasons, the engine ships the grammar's initial bitmask row for
every position flagged with the reasoning-end token id; the worker applies that row only at
the position whose input token is the marker and ends the sampled window there, so the token
after the marker is always grammar-constrained and the engine resumes its synchronous
handshake from a consistent grammar state. Requests whose reasoning parser has no
recognisable end token keep the stock synchronous scheduling.

